#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547107} :activity/id #uuid "971df015-fdeb-53e7-b63b-cd76d289871b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048972 :topic/id #uuid "d04b4eec-dbc6-5996-ac55-c66543b6de53" :topic/math-academy-id 3380} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547105 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b59bf81-336b-5b77-a5ab-993e5da369cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$y$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401393} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2b1eac-ff7d-50ba-8e40-931f60aee6f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401394} {:answer/id #uuid "372a272b-dcaf-5ac5-a75d-2792619b147e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2x$ and $2y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401395} {:answer/id #uuid "92eb8681-34a4-5d3f-9b19-cabe81d62073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2x$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401396} {:answer/id #uuid "eda1895b-7d76-519b-8bab-824851119ae2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401397}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401395} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ec7c466-5f47-5c18-934e-2ba7c46ca3fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8afa6e7b-08b3-5fd6-b960-921c9d4bc534" :question/math-academy-id "q-162811" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z$ on the intersection of the surfaces ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ and ${y}^{2}+z=9$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨2x,\\,2yz,\\,{y}^{2}⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,1⟩ \\\\ {x}^{2}+{z}^{2} &=1 \\\\ {y}^{2}+z &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={y}^{2}+z=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=9\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2x,\\,2yz,\\,{y}^{2}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,\\,0,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨0,\\,2y,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "3e118f3f-9127-5ca6-ad2b-990935fa708c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547107 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172382 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d9268e23-f546-54e0-b2db-acf77c82b969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169156} {:answer/id #uuid "f4714de0-2de3-50b5-8f8b-4c9317b0ac8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169157} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e5d0af-007d-5268-81bb-b5d25ee9af59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169158} {:answer/id #uuid "355cb361-2b35-5c63-afc5-b2b40c97f410" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169159} {:answer/id #uuid "7d31f461-f500-5aca-80bc-de96d84f3b66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169160}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186169158} :answer-field/id #uuid "1228fa7c-87d5-5c53-baa3-b516657b8840" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169155}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "332ef138-daaa-584b-88e1-280c181c6e29" :question/math-academy-id "q-162669" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=2x-2y+z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y+z=1$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=2x-2y+z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y+z=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2 &=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ -2 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ 1 &=\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ y &=-\\frac{1}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{1}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=\\pm 1,\\,y=∓1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+z & =1 \\\\ 1-(-1)+z & =1 \\\\ 2+z & =1 \\\\ z & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+z & =1 \\\\ -1-1+z & =1 \\\\ -2+z & =1 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,-1,-1),\\,(-1,1,3).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,-1,-1)$ | $(-1,1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $3$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(-1,1,3),$ and its minimum value is $-1.$"} :step/id #uuid "42f100b7-8fb4-59ef-8308-9bd4a9fb3bdf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547169}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363493 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1f02490-e174-514c-b237-addfc0f36652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$xy,\\,$ $-y,\\,$ and $2x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363495} {:answer/id #uuid "e1fb72e2-a910-5b6a-b4ad-8e9519cebcfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2y,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $2y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363496} {:answer/id #uuid "db6369f2-9d17-5590-b34f-390179ac9dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2xy,\\,$ $-2x,\\,$ and $4y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363497} {:answer/id #uuid "b52f84e9-49d4-58f3-a677-e6e6618a94b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2x,\\,$ $0,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363498} {:answer/id #uuid "17e269ba-5330-54f6-8386-9e8f93a11f39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$2y,\\,$ $2x,\\,$ and $2z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363497} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31aec0c-583a-5055-888d-4c97b54a776b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186363494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a45a55dd-96e0-52af-8d8a-8615ecb90040" :question/math-academy-id "q-162813" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the maximum of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{z}^{2}$ on the intersection of the surfaces $y-{x}^{2}=1$ and ${x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨\\,,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2z⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨\\,,\\,1,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,,\\,2z⟩ \\\\ y-{x}^{2} &=1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y+{z}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=y-{x}^{2}=1,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=1 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=4\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2xy,\\,{x}^{2},\\,2z⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨-2x,\\,1,\\,0⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,\\,4y,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6a1d79c3-6996-57c6-bc70-9f767ede18f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547149}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547149 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a800fbf-d667-517e-b0be-8ddfb0053d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493435} {:answer/id #uuid "7125e289-4617-5f04-9b31-2d782200a923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493558} {:answer/id #uuid "ab8b12f1-7c38-5f23-a15e-1ff64cbf0b80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493660} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0848f9-3de1-5239-ac6f-93ab9d8c261d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493666} {:answer/id #uuid "e65a77b0-99d4-5d1a-949f-8c30e9525e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493759}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493759} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeb06b96-cbcf-5cb8-b34d-617abe9e3c88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186493884}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e3b568f7-cd27-5a4c-97e4-f5b7033e39b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-162830" :question/problem "Use the method of Lagrange multipliers to find the maximum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=4x+2y-z$ on the intersection of the plane $x-y-z=2$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the maximum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+2y-z\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=x-y-z=2,\\,h(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x,y,z)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y,z)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=2\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe start by expanding the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,2,-1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨1,-1,-1⟩+\\mu ⟨2x,2y,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}4 &=\\lambda +2\\mu x \\\\ 2 &=-\\lambda +2\\mu y \\\\ -1 &=-\\lambda\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ y &=\\frac{3}{2\\mu } \\\\ \\lambda &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mu $ can't be zero since this leads to a contradiction.\n\nNext, we substitute our expressions for $x$ and $y$ into the second constraint:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}}+\\frac{9}{4{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{9}{2{\\mu }^{2}} & =2 \\\\ {\\mu }^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ \\mu  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $x=y,$ we have\n\n$$\n(x,y)=(1,1)\\,\\text{or}\\,(x,y)=(-1,-1).\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $x$ and $y$ into the first constraint:\n\n- If $x=1$ and $y=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ 1-1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x=-1$ and $y=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y-z & =2 \\\\ -1-(-1)-z & =2 \\\\ -1+1-z & =2 \\\\ z & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(1,1,-2),\\,(-1,-1,-2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y,z)$ | $(1,1,-2)$ | $(-1,-1,-2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y,z)$ | $8$ | $-4$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(1,1,-2),$ and its maximum value is $8.$"} :step/id #uuid "c70ffcd4-c208-5a87-8e66-2f7bd16f3515"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547169 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172384 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14de2753-c73d-55f7-8bc7-5fae82ecc1e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6y\\,$ and $0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512143} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbe397f-fccb-5393-89f9-49deddcc70fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3y\\,$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512267} {:answer/id #uuid "91d37c9a-fa0d-55c8-8aed-0c8bcbdb8e4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3{y}^{2}\\,$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512445} {:answer/id #uuid "be7ff705-7435-5e6b-8ed0-6f09f99cd7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6y\\,$ and $y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512556} {:answer/id #uuid "e65e780e-ffad-5628-b3c2-57fcf167b02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$3{y}^{2}\\,$ and $z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512650}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512143} :answer-field/id #uuid "638d1388-9a4e-5ae4-8bcd-3602b1fb5c0c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186169167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "773e8a5f-acc5-5d6b-95d3-53e47a7f0361" :question/math-academy-id "q-162671" :question/problem "Suppose we would like to find the minimum value of the function $f(x,y,z)=x+{y}^{2}+z$ on the intersection of the surfaces $3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ and $x+{z}^{2}=1$ using the method of Lagrange multipliers. Then, the corresponding Lagrange multipliers $\\lambda $ and $\\mu $ satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}⟨1,\\,2y,\\,1⟩ &=\\lambda ⟨0,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,\\bbox[2pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AA}},\\,2z⟩ \\\\ 3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} &=2 \\\\ x+{z}^{2} &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing expressions?" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the minimum value of \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=x+{y}^{2}+z\n$$\n\n \n\nsubject to the constraints \n\n$$\ng(x,y,z)=3{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2,\\,h(x,y,z)=x+{z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) &=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ g(x,y,z) &=2 \\\\ h(x,y,z) &=1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now expand the first equation of the system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z)+\\mu ∇h(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x},{f}_{y},{f}_{z}⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x},{g}_{y},{g}_{z}⟩+\\mu ⟨{h}_{x},{h}_{y},{h}_{z}⟩ \\\\ ⟨1,\\,2y,\\,1⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨0,\\,6y,\\,2z⟩+\\mu ⟨1,\\,0,\\,2z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "eabab0f7-0351-5fd6-8745-4640d71fe038" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186547105}}] :activity/title "Lagrange Multipliers With Multiple Constraints" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186546246}]}
